  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 PM CDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nCurrently there is the remnants of an MCS in southern Canada \nmoving eastward. Meanwhile, weak elevated showers are starting \nto form across a stalled boundary in the south. The noon SPC \nupdate has trimmed the Slight Risk somewhat across the area as \nthis morning's CAMs have not been as aggressive with \nthunderstorm development this afternoon and evening. Previous \nCAMs had the Canadian MCS being the nudge for convection in the\nnorth later this afternoon, with thunderstorms then pushing \neast and southward through the evening. This MCS may be a touch \nfurther north than previous forecast which could limit this \nscenario from playing out. Additionally, lower CAPE values in \nthe north are being observed yet strong shear is still in place.\nIf a thunderstorm can develop in the north, skinny CAPE \nprofiles will limit the severe threat despite higher amounts a \nshear. If these northern storms can develop late this afternoon,\nthen monitoring their progressing south and eastward will be \nworthwhile as they enter into a more unstable area with modest \nto high shear. If this MCS does not kick off convection this \nafternoon, then the area will have to wait for the associated \nmid level wave to move through this evening and possibly \ntonight and interact with the mentioned stalled boundary. There\nis high uncertainty this wave would be enough to overcome some \ncapping in place. Thus the severe threat at least through this\nevening remains conditional and will have to continue to \nmonitor. There still remains enough shear and decent hodographs\nfor rotating cells this evening, thus kept the hazards the same\nfor messaging. Tonight, elevated instability continues to \nincrease while shear remains modest to high. The stalled \nboundary may get pushed slightly eastward as the mentioned mid \nlevel wave move through. This could bring some isolated to \nscattered showers and thunderstorms as a result. CAPE profiles\nare somewhat skinny, although high amounts of shear could bring\nsome isolated severe hail through the night. High DCAPE and 0 to\n3 KM shear could also keep the 70 mph wind threat through the\nnight. This still remains dependent on enough lift to initiate\nthunderstorms, which remains uncertain at this time. Otherwise\nlook for lows tonight in the 50s.\n\nMonday and Tuesday are similar weather days as the west\nnorthwest flow aloft remains along with the mentioned stall\nfrontal boundary as well as an inverted surface trough in the\nwest. The result will be similar, near normal, temperatures \neach day in the mid 70s to mid 80s and lows in the 50s. \nDepending on timing of weak waves in this flow combined with \ninstability along the stalled boundary, near daily chances for \nshowers and thunderstorms are possible each day. SPC also \nshowing an isolated severe thunderstorm risk across the south \neach day mainly where the better instability and shear line up. \nDamaging winds and large hail will be the main threat with any \nisolated thunderstorms to start the week. Westerly flow aloft \ncould also linger wildfire smoke aloft early to at least mid \nweek. Patchy smoke at the surface may be found during the day as\na result. \n\nMid to late week, the trend remains for a cooling trend with\ncontinued near daily chances for showers and thunderstorms. Some\nmodel runs bringing increased precipitation across the area with\nthese shower and thunderstorm chances. This could stem from\nincreased PWAT values as a surface low in the central plains\nattempts to bring increased gulf moisture. Meanwhile the flow\naloft could switch to southwest, bringing a more unsettled\npattern. The concerns with this are some clusters showing the\nmoisture gets cut off to our south and/or the unsettled\nsouthwest flow pattern does not fully materialize. This would\nbring a less active forecast and perhaps not as much of a cool\ndown. Something to monitor going forward. Either way this\npattern would also bring less fire weather concerns for the\nupcoming week given increased RH values, cooler temperatures,\nlight winds, and chances for precipitation.",
